.TH CVEC_DECLARE_SCOPED 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cvec_declare_scoped \- declare a vector variable destroyed automatically at scope exit
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cvector.h>

cvec_declare_scoped(v, type);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This macro declares a vector variable with the name v, for elements of the
given type. It does this exactly as cvec_declare(3) does. The macro also
adds a cleanup handler. That handler destroys the vector when control
leaves the scope around it. The macro creates no vector. It initializes v
to NULL, and v stays NULL until cvec_init(3) or cvec_init_mp(3) gives it a
vector.

The NULL value in v is what makes the cleanup handler safe here. The
handler runs on every exit from the scope. This includes an exit before
anything assigns the variable. A scoped declaration that left the variable
indeterminate therefore gives an arbitrary value to the cleanup handler.
This happens on any early return between the declaration and the creation.
Both companion type variables start at NULL for the same reason. Nothing in
the block then reads an indeterminate object.

The destruction happens on every path out of the block. Such a path is the
closing brace, a return, a break, a continue, or a goto out of the block.
The macro sets the variable to NULL as it destroys the vector. An explicit
call to cvec_destroy(3) earlier in the block is therefore safe, and it
frees nothing twice.

Use cvec_construct_scoped(3) or cvec_construct_mp_scoped(3) instead when
you can create the vector on the same line as the declaration.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
v     Name of the vector variable to declare
type  Element type, for example int, double or struct foo
      Any type name is accepted, including a function
      pointer type such as void (*)(int) and an array
      type such as int[4]. For an array element type,
      give cvec_push(3) an array or a string literal.
      The macro copies its bytes into the element
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro is built on __attribute__((cleanup)), so it needs GCC or
Clang. Use cvec_declare(3) with an explicit cvec_destroy(3) on a
toolchain that does not have that attribute.
The macro expands to a sequence of declarations. It needs a semicolon at
the end of the call site. It therefore belongs where declarations are
legal. It cannot be the body of an if or a loop without braces.
A block that leaves v at NULL is correct. The cleanup handler does
nothing for a NULL vector.
The macro does not free the elements one by one when the vector holds
pointers to heap buffers. Free the element data before you leave the
scope.
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
The vector must not live longer than its scope. Do not return v. Do not
store it anywhere that code reads after the end of the block. Use
cvec_declare(3) when the ownership must leave the block.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
void load(bool use_pool, ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *pool) {
    cvec_declare_scoped(vec, int);

    if (use_pool) {
        cvec_init_mp(vec, pool);
    } else {
        cvec_init(vec);
    }

    cvec_push(vec, 42);
}   /* vec is destroyed here, and on every earlier exit from the block */
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cvec_declare (3),
.BR cvec_construct_scoped (3),
.BR cvec_construct_mp_scoped (3),
.BR cvec_init (3),
.BR cvec_init_mp (3),
.BR cvec_destroy (3)
